WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.160
We had a bunch of interesting elections yesterday,
a very important reminder, a useful reminder

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.990
that we don't just have a presidential race
going on right now.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:12.340
We have 435 House seat elections coming up
in November.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:15.360
Many primaries for those happening in the
months leading up.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.820
We have 35 Senate elections and the possibility
that Democrats would be able to take over

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:20.900
the Senate.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:21.950
We've looked at that.

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:26.860
And yesterday there were a bunch of interesting
primaries that I want to talk about today.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:27.860
Not at all.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:33.870
Surprisingly, Congresswoman Alexandria Cosio
Cortez had a primary challenger who she easily

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:34.870
defeated.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:39.850
The challenger was Michelle Caruso-Cabrera,
a CNBC host, former Republican, running now

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.789
as a Democrat, and AOC easily won.

00:00:42.789 --> 00:00:49.539
As of right now, looks like AOC had about
73 percent of the vote in that race because

00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:51.489
that's such a blue district.

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:56.230
The primary is essentially the general election,
like there will be a Republican on the ballot

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.420
against AOC in November.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:04.710
But the fact that she won the primary all
but guarantees that AOC will get a second

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.229
term in the House of Representatives.

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:12.649
Another race that we were watching very closely
and we'll continue to watch closely now that

00:01:12.649 --> 00:01:18.609
we have a primary winner is the Democratic
challenger against Senate Majority Leader

00:01:18.609 --> 00:01:21.200
Mitch McConnell for his Kentucky Senate seat.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:29.439
Mitch McConnell has been one of the most successful
Republicans from the measurement, from the

00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:36.670
standpoint of what he's been able to achieve,
as many of you know, an excellent and effective

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:43.960
tool for the right in getting a bunch of judges
confirmed, holding off, holding open a seat

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:50.060
on the Supreme Court that should have been
Barack Obama's to replace with Merrick Garland,

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:56.340
of obstructing Mitch McConnell that seat for
long enough that Donald Trump ended up getting

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.340
to choose.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:02.899
Neil Gorsuch, an incredible coup victory for
Republicans, horrible for the world, horrible

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:03.899
for the country.

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:05.399
Never should have been allowed to happen.

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:08.990
But if you're a Republican, certainly Mitch
McConnell has been effective.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:11.510
He has a serious primary challenge.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:16.350
And Amy McGraths seemed to be the leader going
into the primary over the last couple of weeks.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:21.300
There was some energy around Charles Booker,
a more progressive alternative in the Democratic

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.300
primary.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:28.130
But indeed, Amy McGrath has won there with
about 45 percent of the vote.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.890
That's a plurality.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:33.920
Charles Booker only with about 36 percent
of the vote.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.400
So we will see.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.960
Amy, Magrath challenge.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.230
Mitch McConnell, we've said before it's a
very uphill battle.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:46.620
But even getting Mitch McConnell to have a
fight, even forcing Republicans nationally

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.980
to have to spend money to defend Mitch McConnell's
seat is great.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:52.310
And she could actually win.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:57.680
Amy McGrath was up by one point, a tie within
the margin of error, statistically speaking,

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.570
a couple of weeks ago.

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:02.150
That's going to be a great one to watch.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:08.730
Interesting Republican primary in North Carolina
to mention where a 24 year old guy defeated

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:14.810
the Trump endorsed candidate in the Republican
primary for a House seat.

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.840
His name is Madison Cawthorn.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.960
Trump endorsed his opponent, Linda Bennett.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.250
This is the 11th Congressional District in
North Carolina.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:29.460
That's the seat that was formerly held by
Trump's now latest chief of staff.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.170
Mark Meadows, who's a horrible, horrible guy.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:38.990
Unofficially, Cawthorn won very easily by
about seventy five to 25 margin.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:42.630
This is a notable defeat for the Trump endorsed
candidate.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:47.890
But it's important to remember that the guy
that won Cawthorn, he also loves Trump.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:51.760
Trump endorsed his opponent.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.540
But this is not like an anti Trump Republican.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.540
That one.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:56.540
Katherine loves Trump.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.060
It's just that Trump picked somebody else.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:03.520
So either way, if he wins in November, it's
still horrible.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.060
Interestingly, he's not even old enough to
serve as of right now, but because he will

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:14.050
be 25 by the time he would be inaugurated,
he's allowed to be on the ballot.

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:19.230
He will go on to face the Democratic candidate,
Mo Davis, and that's a Republican leaning

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:20.230
district.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:25.720
So that is sort of background level at a base
level that that is one that favors Republicans.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.830
There are four congressional candidates who
support Medicare for all who won primaries

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:36.820
yesterday, Richie Torez, Jamal Bowman, Monder
Jones and Dana Baltar's.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.410
So that's interesting and important.

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.392
And we'll see what happens with their races
in November.

00:04:41.392 --> 00:04:45.140
And then a really interesting story out of
Virginia.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.130
Dr Cameron Webb, I call him Webb, M.D.

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:49.130
That's how I refer to him.

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:56.200
But officially, Dr Cameron Webb, he won the
Democratic primary in Virginia's 5th District

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.190
and he will probably face off against Republican
Bob.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:00.190
Good.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:01.190
In November.

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:05.690
That would be a really great seat to turn
from red to blue.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:09.130
Webb ran hugely on affordable health care.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:10.190
He's a physician.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:12.960
He was leading with 68 percent of the vote.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:13.960
Last I checked.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.440
So that's a big win for him.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:21.430
That actually is a traditionally Republican
district, which is flippable.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.880
And Virginia has been a what I call it.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:29.060
Virginia has been a realistic focus of going
blue.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:35.570
We've seen many elements of Virginia go blue
over the last eight, 10 years.

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:40.389
And officially, that race leans Republican,
but it is a winnable seat.

00:05:40.389 --> 00:05:43.330
And we'll just have to see how the campaign
shakes out.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:44.720
It's a wild district.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:52.200
In that last election cycle, Republican Denver
Riggleman won the seat by about six points

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:57.780
and then was removed by the local Republican
convention because he officiated a gay wedding

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.150
for two of his old friends and campaign staffers.

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:03.180
Which is it?

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.060
It Virginia's been getting pulled blue.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:08.450
It is still Virginia and this stuff still
exists there.

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:10.310
So a number of interesting races.

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:13.370
This ties into how will we vote in November?

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:16.030
The discussions of absentee ballots and vote
by mail.

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:17.950
We'll be talking about that later today.

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:27.450
The desire for scientists and educators and
doctors, at least on the Democratic side,

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:32.650
seems partially to be related to what we've
been seeing with Corona virus and the failed

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:35.350
federal response in the United States.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:37.530
And that's exactly where I want to go next

